# DaoLease — Leases as Facts

Status: proposed design target, derived from and subordinate to
[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). This document defines a vocabulary and
a judging discipline, not a mechanism: it adds no operation to any contract,
no component to any host, and no key to any DaoStream result map.
`dao.stream.md` is unchanged by its existence.

## Precedent

This document is the second instance of a pattern this project already holds,
whose first instance is [`jit-design.md`](./jit-design.md): **advisory streams
with possessor-scheduled application.** There, execution facts are emitted as
trace datoms, never as callbacks; the JIT is advisory — it appends patch
datoms proposing changes and mutates nothing; the VM applies patches only at
explicit safe points it already passes through, on its own schedule; the
negotiation carries an explicit status, so it is itself queryable data; and a
failed guard emits a deopt datom and falls back to baseline.

A lease is that pattern with a different predicate. Renewals are its trace
half: high-frequency evidence emitted by the running party, proposing
nothing. Proposal, acceptance, refusal, and release are its patch half:
negotiation as status facts, advisory until the possessor's own fact makes
them binding. The grantor is the VM: all application happens at points it
already passes through. And the accepted grant's **duration is its guard** —
the predicate *a renewal was observed within the duration* must keep holding,
is checked only at the grantor's judgment points, and its failure is recorded
as a fact followed by fallback to baseline: the resource unheld.

Two things do not carry over, and this document diverges from the precedent
exactly there:

- **A patch is presence; a lapse is absence.** A VM observes a patch
  eventually however lazy its cadence, and lateness only delays a benefit.
  Silence is evidence only over a window the judge actually observed, so this
  document adds an observed-window discipline (see Judging lapse) that the
  JIT never needed — because every JIT mistake is recoverable by deopt, and a
  false lapse has no deopt.
- **Checking the patch stream is optional; judging leases is not.** A VM that
  never applies a patch is a correct, slower VM. A grantor that never judges
  leaks its resource without bound. The obligation lands where possession is
  (see Whose obligation).

## Purpose

A recurring shape in this system is the question *how do I stop caring about
something that may never answer*. A holder is granted something — a pause, a
connection's server-side composition, an entry in a table — and then dies,
hangs, or partitions away without saying so. The grantor must eventually take
the grant back, without detecting death, which is undetectable in general;
without consensus, which an unreachable peer cannot give; and without a stuck
state, which any switch left on becomes.

Java Jini solved this class of problem with the lease, and this document
keeps its insight while rejecting its design. The insight: a grant that
lapses unless renewed inverts the problem — instead of the grantor proving a
holder dead, the holder must keep proving itself interested, and the absence
of proof is the reclaim condition. Silence, the one thing a dead holder
reliably produces, becomes the signal. The design rejected: a lease as a live
object with methods, a synchronous remote `renew`, expiry as absolute time in
the holder's milliseconds, and a background `LeaseRenewalManager` thread —
respectively a value that cannot travel a stream, a host boundary worn as a
function call, two clocks bound that share nothing, and a callback in a
thread. What survives is the concept: time-bounded tolerance, renewed by the
holder, lapsing by silence.

## A lease is facts, accreted

No lease object exists. The record of a lease is its trace on a medium: at
most one fact per negotiation status, any number of renewals, and — in the
common case this vocabulary exists for — no terminal fact from the holder at
all. Each fact is a plain-data value on an ordinary stream; which stream is
the composition's choice, as for every deposited value. Keys live under
`:dao.lease/…`; maps are open in the usual way — a consumer ignores qualified
keys it does not understand.

Following the precedent, the negotiation carries an explicit status, and the
status values whose meaning is shared with `:yin.jit/status` are the same
words. Each status is authored exactly once, by the only party with the
authority to say it:

| Fact | `:dao.lease/status` | Author | Required keys beside `:dao.lease/lease` |
|---|---|---|---|
| Proposal | `:dao.lease/proposed` | holder | — (`:dao.lease/duration` optional: an ask, never a term) |
| Grant | `:dao.lease/accepted` | grantor | `:dao.lease/duration`; `:dao.lease/max` optional |
| Refusal | `:dao.lease/rejected` | grantor | — |
| Release | `:dao.lease/released` | holder | — |
| Reclaim | `:dao.lease/lapsed` | grantor | — (`:dao.lease/cause` optional: silence, cap, policy) |

- `:dao.lease/lease` is the lease's identity: plain data, minted by whichever
  party authors the lease's first fact, unique among that party's leases. A
  grantor may grant unsolicited — a lease whose first fact is `:accepted` —
  because the canonical case, a server leasing a per-connection composition,
  asks nobody first.
- **Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A proposal may carry a
  desired duration; it is advisory, exactly as a patch's replacement block is
  advisory until the VM accepts it. The grant's `:dao.lease/duration` — the
  silence tolerated after each observed renewal — is the grantor's declared
  tolerance, a statement of its nature like a transport's retention policy.
  `:dao.lease/max` optionally caps total consecutive tenure.
- `:dao.lease/lapsed` is appended **when the grantor acts**, never when lapse
  "happens" — it is the deopt datom: the record of a judgment applied, after
  which baseline holds. It also records a grantor ending the lease for
  reasons of its own (shutdown, the cap, policy); a lease is never a promise
  to the holder, only a bound on the grantor's tolerance of silence.
- There is no `:rolled-back` here, deliberately. Rollback undoes an
  application and restores the world as if it had not happened; a lapse ends
  a tenure whose entire past remains valid, and undoes nothing — a reclaim
  has no deopt, which is why the judging discipline below is conservative.
  Conversely `:released` has no patch analog: the JIT never withdraws a
  patch because an unapplied patch costs nothing, whereas a holder done early
  can save the grantor up to one duration of waiting. Release is courtesy,
  not obligation; a system in which no release is ever appended is fully
  correct and merely slower.

**Renewal** is not a status — it is the trace half, distinguished by key as
the precedent distinguishes its streams by namespace:

```clojure
{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal
 :dao.lease/lease <id>}
```

A renewal carries no time and proposes nothing. It says one thing — *I still
want this* — and the fact of it, that it was appended and when it is
observed, is its entire content. A renewal that asked for a new duration
would be negotiating; renegotiation, where a composition wants it, is a new
proposal, not a fat renewal.

## Expiry is an interpretation, applied at the grantor's own points

Nothing fires when a lease lapses. The grantor runs an interpreter that reads
the medium through its own cursor, notes on its own clock when it observes
each renewal, and judges: when now minus the last observed renewal exceeds
the granted duration plus whatever tolerance this judge chooses, the guard
has failed.

The judgment points are the safe-point model of the precedent: points the
grantor's composition **already passes through** — the runs of an interpreter
its runtime already drives. The clock is **read there, never armed**. A timer
that fires is a callback; a clock read at a judgment point is a question
about now, which is the IO Model's third way applied to time. The cost is
the precedent's cost: as a patch waits for the next safe point, a lapse
becomes actionable at the next judgment run, so reclamation latency is
duration plus judgment cadence — eventual, bounded, never instantaneous.

Two interpreters with different tolerances may disagree about whether a
lease has lapsed, and neither is wrong. What resolves the disagreement is
possession, not truth: the interpreter composed with the resource — the one
that can actually close the socket, stop the forwarder, free the entry — is
the one whose judgment becomes a `:lapsed` fact, and that fact records which
judgment prevailed. Authority follows the resource and never needs the
holder's agreement; that is what reclaiming *without consensus* means.

## Whose clock decides

No absolute time crosses a boundary. A grant carries a duration, a renewal
carries nothing, and an expiry timestamp appears in no fact. The judge's
clock measures intervals between its own observations, so the comparison
happens entirely on one clock and skew cannot enter it. Skew becomes transit
delay, and delay errs safe: a renewal is observed at or after it was
appended, so the grantor tolerates at least as long as the holder expects.
The dangerous direction — reclaim earlier than the holder expects — needs no
clock error, only a slow pipe under the last renewal; a holder therefore
renews at a fraction of the duration (half, as DHCP does) and treats the
duration as its safety bound, never its cadence.

## Judging lapse

A judge keeps, as private interpreter state, the newest renewal observed per
lease and its observation time on the judge's own clock — the analog of the
JIT's profile state: derived from the stream, rebuildable from it, consulted
by nothing else. It is not a registry.

Absence is evidence **only over a window the judge actually observed** — the
discipline the precedent never needed, because a patch is presence and a
lapse is absence, and because a false lapse cannot be deopted. Concretely:
on a `gap`, the judge recovers its cursor and re-reads before renewing any
conclusion — eviction is oldest-first, so if any renewal for a lease is
retained, the newest is. After losing its state — a restart, or a gap
spanning the watched window — a judge knows nothing about silence: it must
wait one full duration from resumed observation before concluding lapse.
The asymmetry is deliberate: a late reclaim costs patience; a false one
costs a live holder its grant. The cap is judged under the same discipline
from the judge's own observation of the grant, with the same conservative
restart.

## Whose obligation

Here this document diverges from its precedent on purpose. A VM that never
checks the patch stream is a correct, slower VM; a grantor that never judges
leaks its resource without bound. The obligation to judge cannot be a
callback and cannot be a component, so it lives in the only two places the
architecture has: **possession** — the leak lands on the grantor's own
resource, so the party with the incentive is the party with the authority —
and **assembly**: a composition that grants leases and wires no judging
interpreter over the medium has not built a slow lease but a leak, and that
is a host assembly defect, refused when the composition is built, exactly as
`dao.stream.ws.md` refuses a reject-mode deposit destination. A grantor's
declared judgment cadence completes the lease's one promise: reclamation
within duration plus cadence.

## What a lease may never do

A lease is never a live object: everything one can do about a lease is
appending a fact or judging one. It never fires: no timer holds a callback,
no notification marks a lapse; a composition that wants to observe reclaims
reads the medium like any other interpreter. It never has a registry: a
shared lease table is hidden global state, and the judge's private derived
view is as far as accounting goes. It never puts absolute time on the wire.
It never erases: a reclaim frees the resource, not the record; the medium's
retention policy is the only eraser, here as everywhere. And it is never
renewed by machinery: a holder renews from its own control flow, at a
cadence its driver chooses, because renewal is evidence the holder is alive,
and a background renewer that outlives its holder's purpose — Jini's
`LeaseRenewalManager` — manufactures exactly the false liveness the lease
exists to end.

## Renewal forever

A hostile holder gains little by renewing forever, and what it gains is
priced. Renewal buys tolerance for silence and nothing else; renewing
forever holds the lease forever, which `:dao.lease/max` bounds — past it the
grantor lapses the lease regardless of renewals, and an honest long-lived
holder proposes anew. Renewal costs presence, the honest price, and a holder
able to pay it indefinitely is indistinguishable from a legitimately
long-lived one; distinguishing them is admission policy — who gets
`:accepted` at all, eventually gated by Shibi — never lease vocabulary.
Whether a leased grant is *dangerous* is judged where it is granted: a
paused DaoStream forwarder retains nothing extra, so that pause harms only
the pauser, while a lease over a genuinely scarce resource needs its cap.
The vocabulary carries the cap; the composition decides it.

## Out of scope

What is leasable is the domain's business. Carriage — which frames or files
carry these facts across a particular boundary — belongs to the transport
documents, beginning with `dao.stream.ws.md`. Cancellation of in-flight work
is untouched: a lease reclaims what silence abandons and cannot retract a
request already made. Transfer or delegation of a lease between holders
waits for a real need. Authorization is Shibi's, reserved. A renewal service
is forbidden above, not deferred.

